  "findings": [
    {
      "heading": "Epidemiological weeks > **lubridate**",
      "segment_index": "S24.P1",
      "kind": "untrue",
      "en_span": "chapters/dates.qmd:566-566",
      "tr_span": "chapters/dates.fr.qmd:576-576",
      "proposition": "The English lists the literal R strings accepted by floor_date()'s unit = argument as \"second\", \"minute\", \"hour\", \"day\", \"month\", \"year\"; the French translates these literal argument values into French words (\"seconde\", \"minute\", \"heure\", \"jour\", \"mois\", \"année\"), which are not valid values of unit =.",
      "reader_consequence": "A French reader who copies the listed values writes floor_date(date, unit = \"seconde\") or unit = \"année\" and gets an error from lubridate, with nothing in the page explaining that the unit strings must be in English.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Convert to Date > **lubridate**",
      "segment_index": "S8.P1",
      "kind": "untrue",
      "en_span": "chapters/dates.qmd:150-150",
      "tr_span": "chapters/dates.fr.qmd:154-154",
      "proposition": "The English says the lubridate helper FUNCTIONS are named after abbreviations of date formats; the French says the SYNONYMS are named after date-format abbreviations (\"les synonymes sont nommés d'après les abréviations des formats de date\").",
      "reader_consequence": "The French reader never learns why the functions are called ymd(), mdy() and dmy(), so the naming rule that lets them pick the right helper for a new date layout is lost, and they are instead told something false about the date synonyms (01 / Jan / January).",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Convert to Date > **lubridate**",
      "segment_index": "S8.P2",
      "kind": "added",
      "en_span": "chapters/dates.qmd:158-158",
      "tr_span": "chapters/dates.fr.qmd:162-162",
      "proposition": "The French adds \"Cette fonction fonctionne avec n'importe quel séparateur utilisé dans les variables\" (ymd() works with ANY separator used in the variables); the English segment states only that ymd() flexibly converts year-then-month-then-day values, and elsewhere claims only \"a variety of separators\".",
      "reader_consequence": "The French reader is told an unrestricted guarantee, so an unusual separator that ymd() cannot parse reads as a bug rather than as an unsupported format, and they will not fall back to as.Date(format = ) or parse_date().",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Date display > Month-Year",
      "segment_index": "S22.P3",
      "kind": "added",
      "en_span": "chapters/dates.qmd:555-555",
      "tr_span": "chapters/dates.fr.qmd:565-565",
      "proposition": "The French adds that scale_x_yearmon() is used \"pour ajuster la façon dont les dates sont affichées sur le graphique\" (to adjust how dates are displayed on the plot); the English only states that zoo offers as.yearqtr() and that scale_x_yearmon() can be used with ggplot(), without stating what it adjusts.",
      "reader_consequence": "Minor: the French reader takes scale_x_yearmon() to be a date-label formatting control, when the English makes no claim about what it does, so they may reach for it instead of scale_x_date(date_labels = ) for display formatting.",
      "confidence": "low"
    }
  ]
